\).\n\n   *Diagonals.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set\n   \\[\n   D_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_c|=c-1 .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\). Note that \\(|D_c|\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) for every \\(c\\).\n\n   *Pairing algorithm.*  \n   Let \\(s\\) satisfy (5). We shall produce \\(s\\) disjoint pairs of points, each pair taken from two different diagonals; the line through each pair will be sunny.\n\n   **Algorithm.**  \n   Initialise the set \\(U:=T_n\\).  \n   Repeat \\(s\\) times:\n   1. Choose the smallest diagonal index \\(c\\) for which \\(U\\cap D_c\\neq\\varnothing\\); pick the point \\(P\\) in this intersection with smallest first coordinate.\n   2. Scan the diagonals in increasing order starting from \\(c+1\\) until a diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) with \\(U\\cap D_{c'}\\neq\\varnothing\\) is found; pick the point \\(Q\\) in this intersection with smallest first coordinate.\n   3. Record the pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) and delete both points from \\(U\\).\n\n   **Why the algorithm never fails.**  \n   Suppose the algorithm stops before completing \\(s\\) iterations. Then after some deletions there exists a diagonal \\(D_{c_0}\\) such that all remaining points lie in \\(D_{c_0}\\). Let \\(r\\) be the number of points already removed; then \\(|U|=|T_n|-2r\\). Because each iteration removes exactly two points, \\(|U|\\) is even. Moreover,\n   \\[\n   |U| \\ge |D_{c_0}| \\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(|D_{c_0}|\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and at most \\(2s\\) points have been removed, we have\n   \\[\n   |U| = |T_n|-2r \\ge |T_n|-2s .\n   \\]\n   Using (5) one checks that \\(|T_n|-2s \\ge \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\) for every admissible \\(s\\) (a short calculation is given below). Hence \\(|U|\\) is strictly larger than the size of any single diagonal, contradicting the assumption that all remaining points lie in one diagonal. Therefore the algorithm can always find a second diagonal and proceeds for all \\(s\\) steps.\n\n   *Explicit verification of the inequality.*  \n   Write \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). For the maximal admissible \\(s\\) we have \\(s=n-2\\) (or the values in (5) for small \\(n\\)). Then\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-2s = \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2(n-2)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-4n+8}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2+2 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}+1\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\), the right‑hand side exceeds \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). The same inequality holds for any smaller \\(s\\) because decreasing \\(s\\) only increases \\(|T_n|-2s\\). ∎\n\n   **Sunny lines are indeed sunny.**  \n   By construction the two points of each pair lie on distinct diagonals, so their sums \\(a+b\\) differ; consequently the slope is neither \\(0\\) (different \\(y\\)-coordinates) nor \\(\\infty\\) (different \\(x\\)-coordinates) nor \\(-1\\) (different sums). Hence each line is sunny.\n\n   **Interaction with horizontal lines.**  \n   The two points of a pair have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so each sunny line removes at most one of the original horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Moreover, distinct sunny lines involve distinct points, so no horizontal line is removed twice. Therefore after drawing the \\(s\\) sunny lines we retain at least \\(n-s\\) horizontal lines, all distinct.\n\n   **Completing the covering.**  \n   The remaining uncovered points are exactly those not belonging to any sunny line; there are \\(|T_n|-2s\\) of them. Each of the retained horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) with that second coordinate, and there are exactly \\(n-s\\) such lines. Since each horizontal line can hold at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2) we have\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n \\ge |T_n|-2s,\n   \\]\n   which is precisely inequality (4) rewritten; it holds because \\(s\\) satisfies (5). Hence the horizontal lines together with the \\(s\\) sunny lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\). All lines are distinct, giving a configuration with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently for every integer \\(s\\) satisfying (5) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge n-1\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then at most one line is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(\\ell\\). By Lemma 2, \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). The sunny lines together contain at most \\(2k\\) points (Lemma 1). Hence\n   \\[\n   |T_n|\\le 2k+n\\le 2(n-1)+n=3n-2 .\n   \\]\n   Compare with \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). For \\(n\\ge5\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2\\), a contradiction.  \n\n   For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|T_4|=10=3\\cdot4-2\\). If a configuration with three sunny lines existed, the remaining line would have to contain exactly \\(n=4\\) points of \\(T_4\\).  \n\n   **Lemma 3 (Lines with four points of \\(T_4\\) are horizontal or vertical).**  \n   The set \\(T_4\\) consists of the ten points\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n   \\cup\\{(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Any line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can contain at most two of these points by Lemma 1. A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(c-1\\le3\\) points because \\(c\\le5\\). Hence a line containing four points must be horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)). ∎\n\n   Thus the non‑sunny line would be horizontal or vertical, contradicting the assumption that it is the *only* non‑sunny line (the horizontal/vertical family is already counted among non‑sunny lines). Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound (5) with the explicit construction for every admissible value yields the exact set of possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\} & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4\\} & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\} & n=5,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\} & n\\ge6 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n\nFor each admissible \\(k\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no configuration exists for any larger \\(k\\)."
