s is established.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Define the required point set**\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\)).**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, \\(q>0\\), and \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   Assuming three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in T_n\\) lie on the line, the differences satisfy\n   \\[\n   (x_i-x_j)p=(y_i-y_j)q\\qquad (i\\neq j). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(q\\mid (x_i-x_j)\\) and \\(p\\mid (y_i-y_j)\\) for all pairs.  \n   Because all coordinates lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), we have \\(|x_i-x_j|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|y_i-y_j|\\le n-1\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   |p|\\le n-1,\\qquad |q|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Order the three points so that \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\). From (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_2-x_1\\equiv0\\pmod q,\\qquad x_3-x_2\\equiv0\\pmod q,\n   \\]\n   so the three \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with common difference a positive multiple of \\(q\\).  \n   Since the whole progression lies in \\([1,n]\\), the only possibilities are\n   \\[\n   x_2-x_1 = q,\\; 2q,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The same holds for the \\(y\\)-coordinates, whose common difference is a positive multiple of \\(|p|\\).\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(|p|\\ge2\\) and \\(|q|\\ge2\\).  \n   Then the common differences of the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-progressions are at least \\(2\\). Consequently the slope\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y_3-y_1}{x_3-x_1}\n   \\]\n   satisfies \\(|m|\\ge1\\). Because the three points belong to the triangle\n   \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\), the line segment joining the extreme points \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_3,y_3)\\) must intersect the line \\(a+b=n+1\\) before reaching a third lattice point. A direct computation shows that the third lattice point would have to satisfy\n   \\[\n   x_2 = x_1 + q,\\qquad y_2 = y_1 + p,\n   \\]\n   and then\n   \\[\n   x_2+y_2 = (x_1+y_1)+(p+q).\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign (otherwise the three points could not be ordered with increasing \\(x\\)), we have \\(p+q\\ge2\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   x_2+y_2 \\ge (x_1+y_1)+2 .\n   \\]\n   Starting from the smallest possible sum \\(x_1+y_1\\ge2\\), after two steps the sum already exceeds \\(n+1\\) when \\(n\\le3\\); for larger \\(n\\) the same inequality forces \\(x_2+y_2>n+1\\) because the maximal allowed sum is \\(n+1\\). Thus the middle point cannot belong to \\(T_n\\), a contradiction.\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(|p|=1\\) or \\(|q|=1\\).  \n   If \\(|p|=1\\) then the slope is \\(\\pm1/q\\). The condition \\(m\\neq-1\\) excludes the case \\(p=-q\\). If \\(|q|=1\\) then the slope is an integer different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\). In both sub‑cases the three points would have to lie on a line of slope \\(\\ge1\\) or \\(\\le-1\\), and the same argument as in Case 1 shows impossibility.\n\n   Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\); it can contain at most two. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)).**  \n\n   *Horizontal* \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the line meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points.  \n   *Vertical* \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   *Diagonal* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): the integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.  \n   Any other line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); by Lemma 1 it can contain at most two points, certainly \\(\\le n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Counting bound without double‑counting.**  \n   Order the \\(n\\) lines arbitrarily and process them one by one, each time counting only the points of \\(T_n\\) that have not appeared on earlier lines.  \n   *A sunny line* contributes at most two *new* points by Lemma 1.  \n   *A non‑