 with slope magnitude \\(\\ge1\\) inside the triangle cannot contain three interior lattice points because the third point would necessarily lie outside the triangle. The solution merely states \u201ca direct computation shows \u2026\u201d without presenting it.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Computation of the middle point\n> \u201cA direct computation shows that the third lattice point would have to satisfy \\(x_2 = x_1 + q,\\ y_2 = y_1 + p\\), and then \\(x_2+y_2 = (x_1+y_1)+(p+q)\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe \u201cdirect computation\u201d is not displayed. One must argue that, given the arithmetic progressions, the middle point must be exactly one step from the first point, i.e., the smallest possible step, otherwise the three points would not be consecutive in the progression. This needs clarification.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Inequality leading to contradiction\n> \u201cSince \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign \u2026 we have \\(p+q\\ge2\\). Hence \\(x_2+y_2 \\ge (x_1+y_1)+2\\). Starting from the smallest possible sum \u2026 after two steps the sum already exceeds \\(n+1\\) when \\(n\\le3\\); for larger \\(n\\) the same inequality forces \\(x_2+y_2>n+1\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe argument mixes cases \\(n\\le3\\) and \\(n>3\\) without a unified inequality. Moreover, the statement \u201cthe same inequality forces \\(x_2+y_2>n+1\\) because the maximal allowed sum is \\(n+1\\)\u201d needs a precise bound: one must show that the minimal possible \\(x_1+y_1\\) is at least \\(2\\), so adding \\(2\\) yields at least \\(4\\), which may still be \u2264\\(n+1\\) for larger \\(n\\). The proof does not handle the general range of \\(n\\) rigorously.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(|p|=1\\) or \\(|q|=1\\)\n> \u201cIf \\(|p|=1\\) then the slope is \\(\\pm1/q\\). \u2026 In both sub\u2011cases the three points would have to lie on a line of slope \\(\\ge1\\) or \\(\\le-1\\), and the same argument as in Case\u202f1 shows impossibility.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe reduction to the previous argument is not justified: when \\(|p|=1\\) the slope magnitude may be less than \\(1\\) (e.g., \\(p=1, q=3\\) gives slope \\(1/3\\)). The claim that the slope is \u201c\\(\\ge1\\) or \\(\\le-1\\)\u201d is false in general, so the argument from Case\u202f1 does not directly apply. A separate analysis is required.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Non\u2011sunny lines\n> \u201cHorizontal \u2026 contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points. \u2026 Any other line \u2026 can contain at most two points, certainly \\(\\le n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct, assuming Lemma\u202f1 is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Counting bound (4)\n> \u201cOrder the \\(n\\) lines arbitrarily \u2026 a sunny line contributes at most two *new* points \u2026 a non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points \u2026 \\(|T_n|\\le 2s+(n-s)n\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe bound presumes that after processing some lines, a later non\u2011sunny line can still contribute up to \\(n\\) *new* points, i.e., that the points it would cover have not already been used by earlier sunny lines. This is not guaranteed; earlier sunny lines could have already taken points that lie on the later non\u2011sunny line, reducing its contribution. The solution does not argue why the worst\u2011case (maximal) contribution is still attainable for the purpose of an upper bound. A rigorous proof would need to show that the bound is indeed an upper bound regardless of ordering, perhaps by a double\u2011counting argument, which is omitted.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Derivation of inequality (5)\n> \u201cSubstituting \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) yields \\(s\\le\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2\\,(n-2)}\\) \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Algebraic manipulation is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 15. Construction \u2013 Base covering\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\); this gives \\(s=0\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 16. Definition of diagonals \\(D_c\\)\n> \u201c\\(D_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}\\), \\(|D_c|=c-1\\). The family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 17. Pairing algorithm description\n> \u201cRepeat \\(s\\) times: pick smallest diagonal with a remaining point, then the next diagonal with a remaining point, pair them, delete both.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe algorithm\u2019s success hinges on the existence of a second diagonal with a remaining point at each iteration. The proof attempts to guarantee this via an inequality, but the argument is not fully fleshed out (see next items).\n\n---\n\n### 18. Argument that algorithm never fails\n> \u201cSuppose it stops \u2026 then all remaining points lie in a single diagonal \\(D_{c_0}\\). \u2026 Using (5) one checks that \\(|T_n|-2s \\ge \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\) for every admissible \\(s\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe inequality \\(|T_n|-2s \\ge \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\) is asserted without a detailed verification. For each \\(n\\) and each admissible \\(s\\) (which varies with \\(n\\)), one must compute the left\u2011hand side and compare it to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\). The solution merely says \u201ca short calculation is given below\u201d but the calculation is not shown. Without this, the claim that the algorithm cannot get stuck is unproven.\n\n---\n\n### 19. Size comparison with a single diagonal\n> \u201cSince \\(|D_{c_0}|\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) \u2026 we have a contradiction.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nEven if \\(|U| > \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), it does not automatically contradict the assumption that all points of \\(U\\) lie in a single diagonal, because the diagonal size bound is \\(\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) *only* for the original diagonal sets; after deletions, the remaining points could be a subset of a diagonal, possibly larger than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) if the original diagonal was larger (e.g., for small \\(c\\)). The argument assumes the worst\u2011case diagonal size is always \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), which is not true for diagonals near the middle of the triangle (they can have size up to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) indeed, but the proof must treat the exact maximum). The reasoning is therefore incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 20. Sunny lines are indeed sunny\n> \u201cTheir sums \\(a+b\\) differ; consequently the slope is neither \\(0\\) \u2026 nor \\(-1\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe implication \u201cdifferent sums \u21d2 slope \u2260 \u20131\u201d needs a brief algebraic justification: if \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) satisfy \\(x_1+y_1\\neq x_2+y_2\\), then \\((y_2-y_1)/(x_2-x_1) = -1\\) would give \\(y_2-y_1 = -(x_2-x_1)\\) \u21d2 \\(x_2+y_2 = x_1+y_1\\), a contradiction. This short argument is omitted.\n\n---\n\n### 21. Interaction with horizontal lines\n> \u201cEach sunny line removes at most one of the original horizontal lines \u2026 no horizontal line is removed twice.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nA sunny line could intersect two different horizontal lines at two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g., a line with slope not 0 could cross \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=5\\) within the triangle). The claim that at most one horizontal line is \u201clost\u201d per sunny line is not justified; a more detailed argument is required to show that the two points of a sunny pair have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, which prevents the line from containing two points on the same horizontal line, but does not prevent it from intersecting two *different* horizontal lines. The solution conflates \u201cremoving a horizontal line\u201d with \u201ccontaining a point on that line\u201d, which needs clarification.\n\n---\n\n### 22. Final covering argument\n> \u201cThe remaining uncovered points are exactly those not belonging to any sunny line; there are \\(|T_n|-2s\\) of them. \