& (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   A line with slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or undefined (vertical) clearly can contain four points (e.g. \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1)\\)).  \n   Consider a line of slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty\\). Write it as \\(y=mx+b\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\). By Lemma\u202f1 such a line can contain at most two points of \\(T_4\\) unless \\(|p|=1\\) or \\(|q|=1\\). If \\(|p|=1\\) then the slope is \\(\\pm1/q\\). The only possibilities with integer points inside the triangle are \\(m=1\\) and \\(m=-1\\). The line \\(y=x\\) meets the set in \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\) but \\((3,3)\\notin T_4\\); actually only \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) belong, so at most two points. The line \\(y=-x+b\\) would be a diagonal of the form \\(x+y=c\\); such a diagonal contains at most \\(c-1\\le3\\) points, never four. If \\(|q|=1\\) then the slope is an integer \\(\\neq0,-1\\). For \\(|m|\\ge2\\) the vertical distance between successive lattice points on the line exceeds the width of the triangle, so at most two points lie in \\(T_4\\). The remaining integer slope is \\(m=1\\), already treated. Hence no non\u2011horizontal/non\u2011vertical line can contain four points. \u220e\n\n   Consequently the unique non\u2011sunny line would have to be horizontal or vertical, i.e. it would belong to the family of non\u2011sunny lines already counted. This would give at least two non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non\u2011sunny. Hence a configuration with three sunny lines does not exist for \\(n=4\\).\n\n   Combining the above, for any \\(n\\ge3\\) the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the bound (4).\n\n7. **Final answer.**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\} & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4\\} & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\} & n=5,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\} & n\\ge6 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a construction has been given, and the impossibility of larger \\(k\\) has been proved."

