nes be \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) in any order. For each line we count only the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that have not appeared on any earlier line.\n\n   *If \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny*, Lemma\u202f1 shows that it contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\) altogether, hence at most two new points.\n\n   *If \\(L_{i}\\) is non\u2011sunny*, Lemma\u202f2 shows that the whole line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); after removing points already covered the number of **new** points can only decrease. Thus a non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points.\n\n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) lines. Summing the contributions gives\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Explicit bound for \\(s\\).**  \n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) into (5) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-s\\,(n-2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   s\\le\\frac{2n^{2}-n(n+1)}{2(n-2)}\n   =\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   The right\u2011hand side is a rational number; we now determine the largest integer not exceeding it.\n\n   *For \\(n=3\\):* \\(\\dfrac{3^{2}-3}{2(1)}=3\\).  \n   *For \\(n=4\\):* \\(\\dfrac{4^{2}-4}{2(2)}=4\\).  \n   *For \\(n=5\\):* \\(\\dfrac{5^{2}-5}{2(3)}=\\dfrac{20}{6}=3\\frac13\\), so the integer bound is \\(3\\).  \n   *For \\(n\\ge6\\):* a short division gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^{2}-n}{2(n-2)} = \\frac{n}{2}+\\frac{1}{n-2}\\;<\\; \\frac{n}{2}+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since the expression is also \\(> \\frac{n}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge6\\), the greatest integer not exceeding it is \\(n-2\\).  \n\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   s\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   3 & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n   4 & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n   5 & n=5,\\\\[2pt]\n   n-2 & n\\ge6 .\n   \\end{cases} \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *Horizontal covering.*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\); this yields \\(s=0\\).\n\n   *Diagonals.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define\n   \\[\n   D_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_{c}|=c-1 .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\{D_{c}\\}\\) partitions \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Pairing algorithm.*  \n   Let \\(s\\) satisfy the bound (6). We shall produce \\(s\\) disjoint pairs of points, each pair taken from two different diagonals; the line through each pair will be sunny.\n\n   **Algorithm.**  \n   Initialise \\(U:=T_{n}\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) do:\n   1. Let \\(c\\) be the smallest index with \\(U\\cap D_{c}\\neq\\varnothing\\); pick the point \\(P\\in U\\cap D_{c}\\) having the smallest first coordinate.  \n   2. Scan the diagonals \\(D_{c+1},D_{c+2},\\dots\\) until a diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) with \\(U\\cap D_{c'}\\neq\\varnothing\\) is found; pick the point \\(Q\\) in this intersection with the smallest first coordinate.  \n   3. Record the pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) and delete both points from \\(U\\).\n\n   **Why the algorithm never fails.**  \n   Assume it stops after \\(t<s\\) iterations. Then after the deletions the set \\(U\\) is non\u2011empty and every remaining point lies in a single diagonal, say \\(D_{c_{0}}\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   |U|=|D_{c_{0}}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed the largest diagonal has size \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\), but after the first iteration the smallest non\u2011empty diagonal has index at least \\(3\\), whose size is at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).)\n\n   On the other hand each iteration removes exactly two points, so after \\(t\\) steps\n   \\[\n   |U|=|T_{n}|-2t\\ge |T_{n}|-2(s-1). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Using \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the maximal admissible \\(s\\) from (6) we compute\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|-2(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2s+2\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2+4 . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   We now verify that the right\u2011hand side of (9) is **strictly larger** than the bound (7) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *If \\(n\\ge6\\):*  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2+4-\\frac{n}{2}\n   =\\frac{n^{2}-5n+10}{2}>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=5\\):* (6) gives \\(s=5\\). Then (9) yields \\(|U|\\ge4\\) while (7) gives \\(|U|\\le2\\), a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(n=4\\):* (6) gives \\(s=4\\). Then (9) yields \\(|U|\\ge4\\) while (7) gives \\(|U|\\le2\\), a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(n=3\\):* (6) gives \\(s=3\\). Then (9) yields \\(|U|\\ge2\\) while (7) gives \\(|U|\\le1\\), a contradiction.\n\n   Hence the algorithm can be performed for all \\(s\\) steps.\n\n   **Sunnyness of the constructed lines.**  \n   The two points of each pair lie on distinct diagonals, so their sums \\(a+b\\) are different. If the line through them had slope \\(-1\\) we would have\n   \\[\n   y_{2}-y_{1}=-(x_{2}-x_{1})\\Longrightarrow x_{2}+y_{2}=x_{1}+y_{1},\n   \\]\n   contradicting the different sums. The slope cannot be \\(0\\) (different \\(y\\)-coordinates) nor undefined (different \\(x\\)-coordinates). Hence each line is sunny.\n\n   Moreover, the two points of a pair have distinct second coordinates, so a sunny line meets at most one of the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Because the pairs are disjoint, two different sunny lines cannot meet the same horizontal line at two distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\); otherwise that horizontal line would contain two points belonging to the same pair, contradicting the construction.\n\n   **Completion of the covering.**  \n   After drawing the \\(s\\) sunny lines, the uncovered points are exactly those not belonging to any pair, i.e. \\(|T_{n}|-2s\\) points. We keep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-s\\); there are \\(n-s\\) of them, and each contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\). Hence they together cover at most \\((n-s)n\\) points. Because \\(s\\) satisfies (6), inequality (5) guarantees\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s,\n   \\]\n   so the remaining horizontal lines indeed cover all uncovered points. All lines are distinct, giving a configuration with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently for every integer \\(s\\) satisfying (6) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all required points and containing exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then at most one line is non\u2011sunny. By Lemma\u202f2 a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); the sunny lines together contain at most \\(2k\\) points (Lemma\u202f1). Hence\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2k+n\\le 2(n-1)+n=3n-2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) we have \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2\\), a contradiction.  \n\n   It remains to treat \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  Here\n   \\[\n   T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   The ten lines determined by any two of these points are exactly\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3,\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\\\\n   &x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   The three sunny lines among them are \\(y=2,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=4\\). Any three sunny lines already contain the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) (each point lies on a unique sunny line), so the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(3\\), which agrees with (6).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Here \\(|T_{4}|=10\\). By (6) the maximal admissible number of sunny lines is \\(4\\). We must show that a configuration with three sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   **Lemma\u202f3.** *Any line that contains four points of \\(T_{4}\\) is either horizontal or vertical.*\n\n   *Proof.*  Write the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with reduced slope \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \n   If \\(|p|\\ge2\\) and \\(|q|\\ge2\\) then Lemma\u202f1 gives at most two points of \\(T_{4}\\).  \n   If \\(|p|=1\\) (the case \\(|q|=1\\) is symmetric) then \\(m=\\pm1/q\\).  \n\n   \u2013 For \\(m=1/q\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\) the lattice points on the line have \\(x\\)-coordinates differing by multiples of \\(q\\); inside the square \\(\\{1,\\dots ,4\\}^{2}\\) at most two such points can appear.  \n\n   \u2013 For \\(m=-1/q\\) the line is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); its intersection with \\(T_{4}\\) is exactly the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\), whose size is at most \\(3\\).  \n\n   If \\(|q|=1\\) then \\(m\\) is an integer \\(\\neq0,-1\\).  \n\n   \u2013 For \\(|m|\\ge2\\) the vertical distance between successive lattice points on the line exceeds the height of the triangle, so at most two points lie in \\(T_{4}\\).  \n\n   \u2013 The remaining integer slope is \\(m=1\\); the line \\(y=x+b\\) meets \\(T_{4}\\) in at most the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n\n   Hence no non\u2011horizontal, non\u2011vertical line can contain four points of \\(T_{4}\\). \u220e\n\n   Returning to the assumed configuration with three sunny lines, the fourth line (the only non\u2011sunny one) would have to contain the remaining points. Since each sunny line contributes at most two points, the non\u2011sunny line must contain at least\n   \\[\n   10-3\\cdot2=4\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_{4}\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f3. Therefore a configuration with three sunny lines for \\(n=4\\) does not exist, and the maximal admissible number of sunny lines is indeed \\(4\\).\n\n8. **Final answer.**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\} & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4\\} & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\} & n=5,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\} & n\\ge6 .\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a construction has been exhibited, and for any larger \\(k\\) the impossibility has been proved."
