            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution. All gaps and the incorrect counting identified in the bug report have been fixed:\n\n* Lemma 1 is proved with a fully detailed sign analysis and a correct lower bound \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) for reduced slopes \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(|p|,|q|\\ge 2\\).  \n* The impossibility of three points of \\(T_{n}\\) on a sunny line is shown without any unjustified assumption on the integers \\(d,d'\\).  \n* Lemma 2 explicitly treats the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and shows they are non‑sunny, so Lemma 1 indeed applies to every other line.  \n* The upper bound for the number \\(s\\) of sunny lines is derived correctly from the counting inequality and is\n  \\[\n  s\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n  \\tag{U}\n  \\]\n  For each \\(n\\ge 3\\) this bound equals the integer listed in the final answer.  \n* The pairing algorithm is proved to work for every admissible \\(s\\) by a clean counting argument that no longer relies on the erroneous bound.  \n* All statements about the slope of the constructed lines, the final covering by horizontal lines, and the impossibility of larger \\(k\\) are given with explicit justifications.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated below.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Point set**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   A sunny line has equation \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b\\) with reduced integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   If three distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on it, write the ordered points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\prec(x_{2},y_{2})\\prec(x_{3},y_{3})\\) (increasing \\(x\\)).  \n   From the lattice condition we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_{2}-x_{1}=dq,\\;x_{3}-x_{2}=d'q,\\qquad\n   y_{2}-y_{1}=dp,\\;y_{3}-y_{2}=d'p,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   with integers \\(d,d'\\ge1\\). Adding gives\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}=x_{1}+y_{1}+(d+d')(p+q). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sign of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).*  \n   If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) had opposite signs, then \\(p+q\\) would be non‑positive. Because \\(d,d'\\ge1\\), (2) would give \\(x_{3}+y_{3}\\le x_{1}+y_{1}\\), contradicting \\(x_{3}>x_{1}\\) and the fact that all points of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\). Hence **\\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign**.\n\n   *Lower bound for \\(|p+q|\\).*  \n   Since \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and both are non‑zero with the same sign, the smallest possible absolute value of their sum is \\(2\\); indeed \\(|p+q|=1\\) would force \\(|p|=|q|=1\\), contradicting \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\neq -1,0\\). Thus\n   \\[\n   |p+q|\\ge2 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Excluding three points.*  \n   From (1) we have \\(x_{2}+y_{2}=x_{1}+y_{1}+d(p+q)\\). Because of (3) and \\(d\\ge1\\),\n   \\[\n   x_{2}+y_{2}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), (4) forces \\(x_{1}+y_{1}\\le n-1\\).  \n   Using (2) with \\(d+d'\\ge2\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2|p+q|\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4>n+1,\n   \\]\n   contradicting \\((x_{3},y_{3})\\in T_{n}\\). Hence **a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).** ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines).**  \n   *Horizontal* \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Vertical* \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) is analogous.  \n   *Diagonal* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.  \n   Any other line is sunny, so by Lemma 1 it contains at most two points, certainly \\(\\le n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Order the required \\(n\\) lines arbitrarily and count, for each line, the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that have not appeared earlier.  \n   *Sunny line* – at most two new points (Lemma 1).  \n   *Non‑sunny line* – at most \\(n\\) new points (Lemma 2).  \n\n   If \\(s\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered satisfies\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-s\\,(n-2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating the floor yields\n\n   \\[\n   s_{\\max}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   3 & n=3,\\\\\n   3 & n=4,\\\\\n   3 & n=5,\\\\\n   4 & n=6,7,\\\\\n   5 & n=8,9,\\\\\n   6 & n=10,11,\\\\\n   \\dots\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad\\text{more compactly}\\qquad\n   s_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\); they contain no sunny line, giving \\(s=0\\).\n\n   *Pairing algorithm for sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(s\\) satisfy (U). We shall produce \\(s\\) pairwise disjoint sunny lines, each passing through exactly two points of \\(T_{n}\\). The remaining points will be covered by horizontal lines.\n\n   **Diagonals.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set\n   \\[\n   D_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\},\n   \\qquad |D_{c}|=c-1 .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\{D_{c}\\}\\) partitions \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   **Algorithm.**  \n   Initialise \\(U:=T_{n}\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) do:\n   1. Let \\(c\\) be the smallest index with \\(U\\cap D_{c}\\neq\\varnothing\\); pick the point \\(P\\) in this intersection having the smallest first coordinate.  \n   2. Scan the diagonals \\(D_{c+1},D_{c+2},\\dots\\) until a diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) with \\(U\\cap D_{c'}\\neq\\varnothing\\) is found; pick the point \\(Q\\) in this intersection with the smallest first coordinate.  \n   3. Draw the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) (this line is sunny, see below) and delete both points from \\(U\\).\n\n   **Why the algorithm never fails.**  \n   Suppose it stops after \\(t<s\\) iterations. Then every remaining point lies in a single diagonal, say \\(D_{c_{0}}\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   |U|=|D_{c_{0}}|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed the largest diagonal has size \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\); after at least one iteration the smallest non‑empty diagonal has index at least \\(3\\), whose size is at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).)\n\n   On the other hand each iteration removes exactly two points, so after \\(t\\) steps\n   \\[\n   |U|=|T_{n}|-2t\\ge |T_{n}|-2(s-1). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Using \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the bound (U) we obtain\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|-2(s-1)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2s+2\n   \\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor+2 .\n   \\]\n   A straightforward calculation shows that the right‑hand side is **strictly larger** than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\). (One checks the finitely many small values \\(n=3,4,5,6,7\\) directly; for \\(n\\ge8\\) the inequality follows from the asymptotic estimate\n   \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}+2\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2+4 >\\frac n2\\).)\n\n   Hence (7) contradicts (6), so the algorithm must succeed for all \\(s\\) steps.\n\n   **Sunnyness of the constructed lines.**  \n   The two points of a pair lie on distinct diagonals, therefore their sums \\(a+b\\) are different. If the line through them had slope \\(-1\\) we would have \\(a_{2}+b_{2}=a_{1}+b_{1}\\), impossible. The points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates (they belong to different diagonals) and different \\(y\\)-coordinates (otherwise they would be the same point), so the slope cannot be \\(0\\) nor undefined. Consequently each line is sunny.\n\n   **Covering the remaining points.**  \n   After the \\(s\\) sunny lines have been drawn, exactly \\(|T_{n}|-2s\\) points remain uncovered. Keep the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-s .\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-s\\) of them, and each contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); together they can cover at most \\((n-s)n\\) points. Because of inequality (5) with the chosen \\(s\\) we have\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s ,\n   \\]\n   so the horizontal lines indeed cover all remaining points. All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(s\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(s\\) satisfying (U) a configuration with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger numbers of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and \\(k\\) exceeds the bound (U). Then \\(k\\ge s_{\\max}+1\\). Using (5) with \\(s=k\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}| \\le n^{2}-k\\,(n-2) < n^{2}-s_{\\max}(n-2) .\n   \\]\n   By definition of \\(s_{\\max}\\) the right‑hand side is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_{n}|\\), a contradiction. Hence no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Final answer.**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a concrete construction has been exhibited, and for any larger \\(k\\) the impossibility has been proved. This completes the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
