x\\)\u2019s and for the \\(y\\)\u2019s gives\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}=x_{1}+y_{1}+(d+d')(p+q). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   **(i) Same sign of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).**  \n   If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) had opposite signs, then \\(p+q\\le0\\). Since \\(d,d'\\ge1\\), (2) would give \\(x_{3}+y_{3}\\le x_{1}+y_{1}\\). But every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) and the ordering \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\prec(x_{3},y_{3})\\) forces \\(x_{3}>x_{1}\\) and \\(y_{3}>y_{1}\\); consequently \\(x_{3}+y_{3}>x_{1}+y_{1}\\), a contradiction. Hence **\\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign**.\n\n   **(ii) Lower bound for \\(|p+q|\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and both \\(p,q\\neq0\\) have the same sign, the only way to have \\(|p+q|=1\\) would be \\((p,q)=(1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\) up to sign, which is impossible (both numbers are non\u2011zero). The next possible absolute value is \\(2\\); indeed \\(|p+q|=2\\) occurs for \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\) or \\((-1,-1)\\), which correspond to slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\). The slope \\(-1\\) is excluded by the definition of a sunny line, while slope \\(1\\) is allowed. Therefore\n   \\[\n   |p+q|\\ge 2 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **(iii) Excluding three points.**  \n   From (1) we have \\(x_{2}+y_{2}=x_{1}+y_{1}+d(p+q)\\). Using (3) and \\(d\\ge1\\),\n   \\[\n   x_{2}+y_{2}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), (4) forces \\(x_{1}+y_{1}\\le n-1\\).  \n   Applying (2) with \\(d+d'\\ge2\\) and (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2|p+q|\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4>n+1,\n   \\]\n   contradicting \\((x_{3},y_{3})\\in T_{n}\\). Hence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (Non\u2011sunny lines contain at most \\(n\\) points).**  \n   *Horizontal* lines \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) contain the points \\((a,c)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Vertical* lines \\(x=c\\) are analogous.  \n   *Diagonal* lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) contain exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.  \n   Any other line is sunny, so by Lemma\u202f1 it contains at most two points, certainly \\(\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let the required configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny. Order the lines arbitrarily and, for each line, count the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that have not appeared on any earlier line.\n\n   * A sunny line contributes **at most two** new points (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   * A non\u2011sunny line contributes **at most \\(n\\)** new points (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered satisfies the *worst\u2011case* inequality\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-s\\,(n-2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\); they contain no sunny line, giving \\(s=0\\).\n\n   *Pairing algorithm for sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(s\\) satisfy (U). We shall produce \\(s\\) pairwise disjoint sunny lines, each passing through exactly two points of \\(T_{n}\\). The remaining points will be covered by horizontal lines.\n\n   **Diagonals.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set\n   \\[\n   D_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\},\n   \\qquad |D_{c}|=c-1 .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\{D_{c}\\}\\) partitions \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   **Algorithm.**  \n   Initialise \\(U:=T_{n}\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) do:\n   1. Let \\(c\\) be the smallest index with \\(U\\cap D_{c}\\neq\\varnothing\\); pick the point \\(P\\) in this intersection having