xpression becomes\n     \\[\n     m(2m+1)-2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+2\n     =2m^{2}+m-2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+2 .\n     \\]\n     If \\(m\\) is even, \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor=m/2\\) and the value is \\(2m^{2}+m-m+2=2m^{2}+2>m\\).  \n     If \\(m\\) is odd, \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor=(m-1)/2\\) and the value is \\(2m^{2}+m-(m-1)+2=2m^{2}+3>m\\).  \n     In both sub\u2011cases the result exceeds \\(m=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n   - **Case \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).**  \n     Then \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m\\).  \n     Compute\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor+2\n     =\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(2m+1)(2m)}{2(2m-1)}\\Bigr\\rfloor+2 .\n     \\]\n     The fraction inside the floor equals\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(2m+1)(2m)}{2(2m-1)}= \\frac{2m^{2}+m}{2m-1}=m+\\frac{m}{2m-1}.\n     \\]\n     Hence the floor is \\(m\\). The whole expression becomes\n     \\[\n     (2m+1)(m+1)-2m+2 = 2m^{2}+3m+1-2m+2 = 2m^{2}+m+3 .\n     \\]\n     Since \\(2m^{2}+m+3>m\\) for all \\(m\\ge1\\), inequality (8) holds.\n\n   Thus (8) is proved, and (7) contradicts (6). Therefore the algorithm succeeds for all \\(s\\) satisfying (U).\n\n   **Sunnyness of the constructed lines.**  \n   The two points \\(P=(a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\(Q=(a_{2},b_{2})\\) lie on distinct diagonals, so \\(a_{1}+b_{1}\\neq a_{2}+b_{2}\\). Consequently the slope cannot be \\(-1\\). Because the points belong to different diagonals they also have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates; hence the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor undefined. Therefore each line is sunny.\n\n   **Covering the remaining points.**  \n   After the \\(s\\) sunny lines have been drawn, exactly \\(|T_{n}|-2s\\) points remain. Choose the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-s .\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-s\\) such lines. Each horizontal line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); together they can cover at most \\((n-s)n\\) points.  \n   Rearranging inequality (5) with \\(s\\) gives precisely\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s .\n   \\]\n   Hence the chosen horizontal lines are sufficient to cover all remaining points. All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(s\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(s\\) satisfying (U) a configuration with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger numbers of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines existed and \\(k>\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). Plugging \\(s=k\\) into the upper\u2011bound inequality (5) would give\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)< n^{2}-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor (n-2)=|T_{n}|,\n   \\]\n   a contradiction. Hence no configuration can have more sunny lines than the bound (U).\n\n7. **Final answer.**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\\,}\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and a constructive family attaining every value have been proved rigorously."

