\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1.**  \n   *A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).*  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)), \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Suppose three distinct points \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2}), (x_{3},y_{3})\\in T_{n}\\) lie on it.  Order them so that\n   \\[\n   x_{1}<x_{2}<x_{3}.\n   \\]\n   Because the line has slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), the differences of the coordinates are\n   \\[\n   x_{2}-x_{1}=d_{1}q,\\qquad x_{3}-x_{2}=d_{2}q,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y_{2}-y_{1}=d_{1}p,\\qquad y_{3}-y_{2}=d_{2}p,\n   \\]\n   with positive integers \\(d_{1},d_{2}\\).  Adding the two equalities for the \\(x\\)’s and for the \\(y\\)’s gives\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}=x_{1}+y_{1}+(d_{1}+d_{2})(p+q). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sign of \\(p+q\\).*  \n   Since the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \n   Moreover \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p,q\\neq0\\); the only way to have \\(|p+q|=1\\) would be \\((p,q)=(1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\), both impossible.  Hence\n   \\[\n   |p+q|\\ge 2. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Deriving a contradiction.*  \n   From (1) with \\(d_{1}+d_{2}\\ge2\\) and (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2|p+q|\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  Consequently (3) forces\n   \\[\n   x_{1}+y_{1}\\le n-3 .\n   \\]\n   Applying (1) with only the first step (\\(d_{1}\\ge1\\)) yields\n   \\[\n   x_{2}+y_{2}=x_{1}+y_{1}+d_{1}(p+q)\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(x_{2}+y_{2}\\ge n-1\\).  But then (3) gives \\(x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge n+3\\), contradicting the defining inequality \\(x_{3}+y_{3}\\le n+1\\).  Therefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2.**  \n   *A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).*  \n   This follows directly from the description of the set: a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\le n\\); the same bound holds for vertical lines, and a line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny.  \n   Counting the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are first covered by each line we obtain\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *Idea.*  Use \\(s\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\); each such line will pass through exactly two points of \\(T_{n}\\) and will be distinct from all others.  The remaining points will be covered by horizontal (non‑sunny) lines.\n\n   *Pairs of points.*  \n   For an integer \\(t\\ge1\\) define\n   \\[\n   P_{t}=(t,\\,t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+1,\\,t+1).\n   \\]\n   Both points belong to \\(T_{n}\\) provided\n   \\[\n   2t\\le n+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 2(t+1)\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   The line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\) has equation \\(y=x\\); its slope is \\(1\\), so it is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  The two points are distinct, and different values of \\(t\\) give disjoint pairs, hence the resulting sunny lines are all distinct.\n\n   *Number of such lines.*  \n   The above construction yields\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   sunny lines.  When \\(n\\) is even, \\(m=n/2-1\\); when \\(n\\) is odd, \\(m=(n-1)/2\\).\n\n   *One extra sunny line when needed.*  \n   The bound (U) equals either \\(m\\) or \\(m+1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   m, & n\\equiv0,1\\pmod 2,\\\\[2mm]\n   m+1, & n\\equiv2\\pmod 2\\text{ and }n\\ge4 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   (A short elementary verification of this equality is left to the reader; it follows from a direct comparison of the two rational expressions.)\n\n   In the case where the bound equals \\(m+1\\) we add one more sunny line of slope \\(1\\) using the pair\n   \\[\n   P_{\\,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}=\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\; \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr),\\qquad\n   Q_{\\,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}=\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1,\\; \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   which is still contained in \\(T_{n}\\) because \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1)\\le n+1\\).  Thus we can realise **any** integer\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering the remaining points.*  \n   After the \\(s\\) sunny lines have been drawn, exactly \\(|T_{n}|-2s\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) remain uncovered.  Choose the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-s .\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-s\\) such lines, each containing at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); together they can cover at most \\((n-s)n\\) points.  Inequality (4) with the chosen value of \\(s\\) is precisely\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s,\n   \\]\n   so the horizontal lines are sufficient to cover all remaining points.  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(s\\) of them (the ones of slope \\(1\\)) are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every admissible \\(s\\) a configuration with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger numbers of sunny lines.**  \n   If a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines existed and \\(k\\) exceeded the bound (U), then (4) would give\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)<n^{2}-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor (n-2)=|T_{n}|,\n   \\]\n   an impossibility.  Therefore no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Final answer**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\\,}\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and a constructive family attaining every value have been proved rigorously.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
