n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *sunny* line) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write a sunny line in reduced rational form\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n   \\]\n   with the restrictions \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   If two distinct lattice points of the line are \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\), then\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k\\,(q,p) \\qquad\\text{for some }k\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the vector \\((q,p)\\) is **primitive**: it is the smallest non\u2011zero integer vector parallel to the line.\n\n   Suppose, for a contradiction, that three distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on the line.  Order them by increasing \\(x\\)-coordinate and denote them \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2}), (x_{3},y_{3})\\).  By (1) there exist positive integers \\(k_{1},k_{2}\\) such that\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k_{1}(q,p),\\qquad\n   (x_{3},y_{3})-(x_{2},y_{2})=k_{2}(q,p).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives\n   \\[\n   (x_{3}+y_{3})-(x_{1}+y_{1})=(k_{1}+k_{2})(p+q). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Moreover \\(k_{1}+k_{2}\\ge2\\).  Hence the absolute change in the sum \\(x+y\\) between the first and the third point is at least \\(2\\):\n   \\[\n   |x_{3}+y_{3}-(x_{1}+y_{1})|\\ge 2. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  Consequently the three numbers\n   \\[\n   s_{1}=x_{1}+y_{1},\\qquad s_{2}=x_{2}+y_{2},\\qquad s_{3}=x_{3}+y_{3}\n   \\]\n   are distinct integers lying in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  By (4) the distance between the smallest and the largest of them is at least\u202f2, so the three values must be **consecutive** integers:\n   \\[\n   s_{2}=s_{1}+1,\\qquad s_{3}=s_{1}+2. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) we have\n   \\[\n   s_{2}-s_{1}=k_{1}(p+q),\\qquad s_{3}-s_{2}=k_{2}(p+q).\n   \\]\n   Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   k_{1}(p+q)=1,\\qquad k_{2}(p+q)=1.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(k_{1},k_{2}\\) are positive integers, the only possibility is \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(k_{1}=k_{2}=1\\).\n\n   Thus the primitive direction vector satisfies \\(|p+q|=1\\).  Because \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are coprime and both non\u2011zero, the only solutions are\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(-1,2),\\;(2,-1),\\;(-2,3),\\;(3,-2),\\dots\n   \\]\n   The first two are excluded (they give horizontal or vertical lines).  For any remaining solution we have \\(|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(|p|\\ge1\\); therefore the step from \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) to \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) changes **both** coordinates by at least\u202f1.  Consequently\n   \\[\n   x_{2}\\ge x_{1}+1,\\qquad y_{2}\\ge y_{1}+1,\n   \\]\n   and similarly for the second step.  Adding the two steps yields\n   \\[\n   x_{3}\\ge x_{1}+2,\\qquad y_{3}\\ge y_{1}+2.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4,\n   \\]\n   contradicting (5) which requires \\(x_{3}+y_{3}=x_{1}+y_{1}+2\\).  This contradiction shows that three points cannot lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (Non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\le n\\).  \n   - Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \n   - Line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny.  \n   Count the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are first covered by each line.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contribute at most\u202f2 new points; by Lemma\u202f2 a non\u2011sunny line can contribute at most\u202f\\(n\\) new points.  Hence\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *5.1 Sunny lines of slope\u202f1.*  \n   For any integer \\(c\\) with \\(-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le c\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   has slope\u202f1, hence is sunny.  The lattice points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying on \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are exactly the pairs\n   \\[\n   (t,\\,t+c),\\qquad (t+1,\\,t+1+c)\n   \\]\n   where \\(t\\) runs over the integers satisfying\n   \\[\n   1\\le t,\\;1\\le t+c,\\;t+1\\le n+1-(t+1+c).\n   \\]\n   The last inequality is equivalent to \\(2t+2c+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-c\\).  Because \\(c\\) lies in the range above, there is at least one admissible \\(t\\); consequently each line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *5.2 Number of available sunny lines.*  \n   The set of admissible constants \\(c\\) has cardinality\n   \\[\n   m:=2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\begin{cases}\n   n, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Hence we can produce up to \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering two fresh points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *5.3 Matching the upper bound.*  \n   A direct computation shows\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\equiv 1\\pmod 2,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\dfrac{n}{2},   & n\\equiv 0\\pmod 2\\text{ and }n\\ge4,\\\\[2mm]\n   0,               & n=3.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   (The equality follows from elementary manipulation of the rational expression; the three cases correspond to the three possible residues of \\(n\\) modulo\u202f2 and the special small value \\(n=3\\).)\n\n   Comparing (6) and (7) we see that the bound (U) never exceeds the number \\(m\\) of slope\u20111 lines that we can actually realise.  Consequently, for **every** integer\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n   \\]\n   we may select \\(s\\) distinct constants \\(c\\) and draw the corresponding sunny lines \\(\\ell_{c}\\).  After this step exactly\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|-2s\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\) remain uncovered.\n\n   *5.4 Covering the remaining points with horizontal lines.*  \n   Choose the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-s .\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-s\\) such lines, each of which is non\u2011sunny.  By Lemma\u202f2 each horizontal line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); together they can cover at most \\((n-s)n\\) points.  Because of inequality (4) with the chosen value of \\(s\\),\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s,\n   \\]\n   the horizontal lines are sufficient to cover all the still uncovered points.  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly the \\(s\\) lines of slope\u202f1 are sunny.\n\n   Hence a configuration with **any** admissible number \\(s\\) of sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger numbers of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines existed and \\(k\\) exceeded the bound (U).  Then inequality (4) would give\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)<n^{2}-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor (n-2)=|T_{n}|,\n   \\]\n   an impossibility.  Therefore no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Final answer*