 line) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write a sunny line in reduced rational form\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n   \\]\n   with the restrictions \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   If two distinct lattice points of the line are \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\), then\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k\\,(q,p) \\qquad\\text{for some }k\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the vector \\((q,p)\\) is primitive: it is the smallest non‑zero integer vector parallel to the line.\n\n   Assume, for a contradiction, that three distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on the line.  Order them by increasing \\(x\\)-coordinate and denote them \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2}), (x_{3},y_{3})\\).  By (1) there exist positive integers \\(k_{1},k_{2}\\) such that\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k_{1}(q,p),\\qquad\n   (x_{3},y_{3})-(x_{2},y_{2})=k_{2}(q,p).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives\n   \\[\n   (x_{3}+y_{3})-(x_{1}+y_{1})=(k_{1}+k_{2})(p+q). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Moreover \\(k_{1}+k_{2}\\ge2\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   |(x_{3}+y_{3})-(x_{1}+y_{1})|\\ge 2. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  Hence the three integers\n   \\[\n   s_{1}=x_{1}+y_{1},\\qquad s_{2}=x_{2}+y_{2},\\qquad s_{3}=x_{3}+y_{3}\n   \\]\n   lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  Because of (4) the smallest and the largest of them differ by at least 2, while the interval contains only \\(n\\) consecutive integers.  Therefore the only way three distinct integers can fit into \\([2,n+1]\\) with a gap of at least 2 is that they are **exactly** the three consecutive integers\n   \\[\n   s_{1}=t,\\qquad s_{2}=t+1,\\qquad s_{3}=t+2\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\(t\\).  (If the gap were larger than 2, we would need at least four distinct integers, which is impossible with only three points.)\n\n   From (2) we have\n   \\[\n   s_{2}-s_{1}=k_{1}(p+q),\\qquad s_{3}-s_{2}=k_{2}(p+q).\n   \\]\n   Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   k_{1}(p+q)=1,\\qquad k_{2}(p+q)=1.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(k_{1}=k_{2}=1\\).\n\n   The condition \\(|p+q|=1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) forces \\((p,q)\\) to be one of the following coprime pairs:\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(-1,2),\\;(2,-1),\\;(-2,3),\\;(3,-2),\\dots\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, solving \\(p+q=\\pm1\\) for integers with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) yields the infinite family \\((p,q)=(m,m\\pm1)\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\).)  \n   The first two possibilities give a horizontal line (\\(p=0\\)) or a vertical line (\\(q=0\\)), which are excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  For any remaining pair we have \\(|p|\\ge1\\) and \\(|q|\\ge1\\); consequently the step from \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) to \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) changes **both** coordinates by at least 1, i.e.\n   \\[\n   x_{2}\\ge x_{1}+1,\\qquad y_{2}\\ge y_{1}+1,\n   \\]\n   and similarly for the second step.  Adding the two steps yields\n   \\[\n   x_{3}\\ge x_{1}+2,\\qquad y_{3}\\ge y_{1}+2,\n   \\]\n   whence\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4,\n   \\]\n   contradicting (5) which requires \\(x_{3}+y_{3}=x_{1}+y_{1}+2\\).  This contradiction shows that three points cannot lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\).  Since \\(c\\ge1\\), we have \\(n+1-c\\le n\\); thus at most \\(n\\) points lie on the line.  \n   - Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   - For a line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(c\\ge2\\) (both coordinates are at least 1).  Moreover any point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore on such a line we must have \\(c\\le n+1\\).  The integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\) of them, and because \\(c\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny.  Arbitrarily order the lines as \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\).  For each line \\(L_{i}\\) count the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that appear **for the first time** on \\(L_{i}\\) (i.e. that are not already contained in any of the earlier lines).  Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is counted exactly once, so\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\bigl|\\;L_{i}\\cap T_{n}\\setminus\\bigcup_{j<i}L_{j}\\;\\bigr|.\n   \\]\n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line contributes at most 2 new points, and by Lemma 2 a non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points.  Hence\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le 2s+(n-s)n = n^{2}-s\\,(n-2). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(s\\).**  \n\n   *5.1 Sunny lines of slope 1.*  \n   For any integer \\(c\\) with\n   \\[\n   -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le c\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   consider the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Its slope is 1, so it is sunny.  The lattice points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying on \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are precisely the pairs\n   \\[\n   (t,\\,t+c),\\qquad (t+1,\\,t+1+c)\n   \\]\n   where the integer \\(t\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   1\\le t,\\qquad 1\\le t+c,\\qquad t+1\\le n+1-(t+1+c).\n   \\]\n   The last inequality is equivalent to \\(2t+2c+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-c\\).  Because of (6) this upper bound is at least \\(1\\), so such a \\(t\\) exists.  Consequently each line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *5.2 Number of available sunny lines.*  \n   The set of admissible constants \\(c\\) in (6) has cardinality\n   \\[\n   m:=2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *5.3 Matching the upper bound.*  \n   A short computation yields the explicit form of the floor in (U):\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   0,               & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ge5\\text{ and }n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\dfrac{n}{2},   & n\\ge4\\text{ and }n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   (Proof: write \\(n=2q\\) or \\(n=2q+1\\) and perform elementary division; the case \\(n=3\\) is checked directly.)\n\n   Comparing (8) with (9) we see that the bound (U) never exceeds the number \\(m\\) of slope‑1 lines that we can actually realise.  Hence for every integer\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n   \\]\n   we may select \\(s\\) distinct constants \\(c\\) and draw the corresponding sunny lines \\(\\ell_{c}\\).  After this step exactly\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|-2s\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_{n}\\) remain uncovered.\n\n   *5.4 Covering the remaining points with horizontal lines.*  \n   Observe that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) has \\(y\\)-coordinate at most \\(n\\).  The sunny lines \\(\\ell_{c}\\) used above have slopes 1, so the two points they cover on each line have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, namely \\(t+c\\) and \\(t+1+c\\).  Because \\(c\\) ranges between \\(-\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\), the largest possible \\(y\\)-coordinate among all points removed by the sunny lines is\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1+\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor = n .\n   \\]\n   Consequently, after deleting the \\(2s\\) points covered by the sunny lines, any uncovered point still satisfies \\(1\\le y\\le n-s\\).  (Indeed, each horizontal line \\(y=j\\) with \\(j\\le n-s\\) loses at most one of its points to a sunny line, because a sunny line contains at most one point with a given \\(y\\)-coordinate.)  \n\n   Therefore the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-s\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   (which are non‑sunny) together contain at least \\((n-s)n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  By inequality (4) with the chosen value of \\(s\\) we have \\((n-s)n\\ge |T_{n}|-2s\\); hence the lines in (11) are sufficient to cover all points left after step 5.3.  \n\n   The total number of lines is \\(s+(n-s)=n\\); exactly the \\(s\\) lines of slope 1 are sunny.  Thus a configuration exists for every admissible \\(s\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger numbers of sunny lines.**  \n   If a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines existed and \\(k\\) exceeded the bound (U), then (4) would give\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)<n^{2}-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor (n-2)=|T_{n}|,\n   \\]\n   a contradiction.  Hence no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Final answer**  \n   The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\\,}\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and a constructive family attaining every value have been proved rigorously."
