ne, the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that appear for the first time on that line; this yields the bound \\(2s+(n-s)n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\\,}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Point set**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *sunny* line) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write a sunny line in reduced rational form\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n   \\]\n   with the restrictions \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   If two distinct lattice points of the line are \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\), then\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k\\,(q,p) \\qquad\\text{for some }k\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the vector \\((q,p)\\) is primitive: it is the smallest non‑zero integer vector parallel to the line.\n\n   Assume, for a contradiction, that three distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on the line.  Order them by increasing \\(x\\)-coordinate and denote them \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2}), (x_{3},y_{3})\\).  By (1) there exist positive integers \\(k_{1},k_{2}\\) such that\n   \\[\n   (x_{2},y_{2})-(x_{1},y_{1})=k_{1}(q,p),\\qquad\n   (x_{3},y_{3})-(x_{2},y_{2})=k_{2}(q,p).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives\n   \\[\n   (x_{3}+y_{3})-(x_{1}+y_{1})=(k_{1}+k_{2})(p+q). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Moreover \\(k_{1}+k_{2}\\ge2\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   |(x_{3}+y_{3})-(x_{1}+y_{1})|\\ge 2. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  Hence the three integers\n   \\[\n   s_{1}=x_{1}+y_{1},\\qquad s_{2}=x_{2}+y_{2},\\qquad s_{3}=x_{3}+y_{3}\n   \\]\n   lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  Because of (4) the smallest and the largest of them differ by at least 2, while the interval contains only \\(n\\) consecutive integers.  Therefore the only way three distinct integers can fit into \\([2,n+1]\\) with a gap of at least 2 is that they are **exactly** the three consecutive integers\n   \\[\n   s_{1}=t,\\qquad s_{2}=t+1,\\qquad s_{3}=t+2\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\(t\\).  (If the gap were larger than 2, we would need at least four distinct integers, which is impossible with only three points.)\n\n   From (2) we have\n   \\[\n   s_{2}-s_{1}=k_{1}(p+q),\\qquad s_{3}-s_{2}=k_{2}(p+q).\n   \\]\n   Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   k_{1}(p+q)=1,\\qquad k_{2}(p+q)=1.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(k_{1}=k_{2}=1\\).\n\n   The condition \\(|p+q|=1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) forces \\((p,q)\\) to be one of the following coprime pairs:\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(-1,2),\\;(2,-1),\\;(-2,3),\\;(3,-2),\\dots\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, solving \\(p+q=\\pm1\\) for integers with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) yields the infinite family \\((p,q)=(m,m\\pm1)\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\).)  \n   The first two possibilities give a horizontal line (\\(p=0\\)) or a vertical line (\\(q=0\\)), which are excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  For any remaining pair we have \\(|p|\\ge1\\) and \\(|q|\\ge1\\); consequently the step from \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) to \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) changes **both** coordinates by at least 1, i.e.\n   \\[\n   x_{2}\\ge x_{1}+1,\\qquad y_{2}\\ge y_{1}+1,\n   \\]\n   and similarly for the second step.  Adding the two steps yields\n   \\[\n   x_{3}\\ge x_{1}+2,\\qquad y_{3}\\ge y_{1}+2,\n   \\]\n   whence\n   \\[\n   x_{3}+y_{3}\\ge x_{1}+y_{1}+4,\n   \\]\n   contradict