he region  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x_{0}+kq,\\;1\\le y_{0}+kp,\\;x_{0}+kq+y_{0}+kp\\le n+1.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3) we obtain the inequalities  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\frac{n+1-(x_{0}+y_{0})}{|p+q|},\\qquad\n   k\\le \\frac{x_{0}-1}{|q|},\\qquad\n   k\\le \\frac{y_{0}-1}{|p|}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the number of points of a sunny line inside \\(T_{n}\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{\\frac{n+1-(x_{0}+y_{0})}{|p+q|},\n                \\frac{x_{0}-1}{|q|},\n                \\frac{y_{0}-1}{|p|}\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular, if \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) then the first term in (5) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); if \\(|p|=|q|=1\\) (i.e. slope \\(\\pm1\\)) the bound becomes \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\), which can be much larger than\u202f2.\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny.  \n   Order the lines arbitrarily and, for each line, count the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that appear for the first time on that line.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 (which remains valid) a non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points.  \n   By the refined estimate (5) a sunny line with parameters \\((p,q)\\) contributes at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   new points, because the first term in (5) is the dominant restriction (the other two terms are never larger than the first one when the line stays inside the triangle \\(T_{n}\\)).  \n\n   Hence\n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\le\n   \\sum_{\\text{sunny }L}\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p_{L}+q_{L}|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n   \\;+\\;(n-s)n .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|p_{L}+q_{L}|\\ge 2\\) for every sunny line, each sunny line contributes at most \\(1+\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) new points.  Therefore a crude but valid inequality is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\