inct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 1 – choose the sunny lines.*  \n   Pick \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\), for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i}=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(P_{i}\\) draw a sunny line that passes through \\(P_{i}\\) and no other point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   This is always possible: choose a slope that is a rational number whose reduced denominator exceeds \\(n\\); then the line meets the integer lattice only at the prescribed point inside the bounded triangle.  \n   Because the slopes are chosen distinct, the resulting \\(k\\) lines are distinct and sunny, and each of them covers exactly one point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – cover the remaining points with two non‑sunny lines.*  \n   The set \\(T_{n}\\setminus\\{P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\}\\) still contains the whole diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(n\\) points and lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (a non‑sunny line).  \n   All points of \\(D\\) are therefore covered by the single non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\) (those not on \\(D\\) and not among the \\(P_{i}\\)) all satisfy \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   They are covered by the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2}:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\); the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\) have already been covered by the sunny lines, but the line \\(y=1\\) also contains the other points \\((k+1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   Thus \\(L_{2}\\) adds at most \\(n-k\\) new points, all of which lie in \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – count the lines.*  \n   We have produced exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{non‑sunny }L_{1})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{non‑sunny }L_{2})\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound (4) with the explicit constructions of step 4, we obtain the exact set of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎"
