\[\nx_{0}+y_{0}+k_{2}|p+q|\\le n+1,\\qquad\nx_{0}+y_{0}+k_{1}|p+q|\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting yields \\((k_{2}-k_{1})|p+q|\\le0\\), impossible because \\(k_{2}\\neq k_{1}\\) and \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_{n}\\); it contains at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((a,c)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); intersecting with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and \\(1\\le b=d-a\\), again at most \\(n\\) lattice points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  \nProcess the lines in an arbitrary order and count, for each line, how many *new* points of \\(T_{n}\\) it contributes (points not already covered by earlier lines).\n\n* a sunny line can contribute at most \\(2\\) new points (Lemma\u202f1);\n* a non\u2011sunny line can contribute at most \\(n\\) new points (Lemma\u202f2).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+n^{2}-kn\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nL_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\\qquad\nL_{j}:\\;y=j\\;(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nAll are distinct, none is sunny, and each contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (Lemma\u202f2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Points not covered by the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) fails to lie on any of the lines above exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom the second inequality we obtain \\(a\\le k\\).  \nConsequently the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each fixed \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) form a consecutive interval of length \\(2\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_{k}| = 2k .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Sunny lines covering \\(S_{k}\\)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}= (i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\qquad\nQ_{i}= (i,\\,n-k+i).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_{k}\\); they are distinct because the second coordinate differs by \\(1\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = \\frac12\\,x +\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr) .\n\\]\n\n* The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n* Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n-k+i-\\frac12+\\frac12 i = n-k+i\\) and \\(y=n-k+i-1\\); thus \\(S_{i}\\) passes through both \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\).  \n* Any other lattice point on \\(S_{i}\\) would have to differ from \\(P_{i}\\) by an integer multiple of the primitive vector \\((2,1)\\). Adding \\((2,1)\\) increases the sum of coordinates by \\(3\\), which would exceed the bound \\(n+1\\); subtracting \\((2,1)\\) would make a coordinate non\u2011positive. Hence \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\) are the only points of \\(T_{n}\\) on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThe intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), so the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Verification of the whole configuration  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \nBy construction:\n\n* every point of \\(S_{k}\\) lies on some sunny line \\(S_{i}\\);\n* every point of \\(T_{n}\\setminus S_{k}\\) lies on one of the non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) obtained from Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 is also sufficient, as shown by the explicit construction above. Therefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
