  They cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) except the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid b\\ge n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A short calculation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{k}= \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n   \\qquad |S_{k}|=2k .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   List the points of \\(S_{k}\\) in the order  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i}=(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\qquad Q_{i}=(i,\\,n-k+i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12\\,x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   By construction \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the two distinct points \\(P_{i},Q_{i}\\).  \n   Any other lattice point on \\(S_{i}\\) would differ from \\(P_{i}\\) by an integer multiple of the primitive vector \\((2,1)\\); adding \\((2,1)\\) raises the sum of coordinates by \\(3\\), which would exceed the bound \\(n+1\\), while subtracting \\((2,1)\\) would make a coordinate non‑positive. Hence \\(P_{i},Q_{i}\\) are the **only** points of \\(T_{n}\\) on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n   The intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) are distinct, so the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n5. **Verification.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies either on one of the horizontal (or diagonal) non‑sunny lines or on one of the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\); thus the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n   Because the construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\), the bound (1) is sharp.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) together with the explicit construction shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\;}.\n   \\]\n\n   This resolves the problem completely. ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not horizontal, not vertical and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n\\]\n\nwith the extra condition \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nIf \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) are two lattice points on the line, then  \n\n\\[\ny_{2}-y_{1}= \\frac{p}{q}(x_{2}-x_{1})\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(q\\mid (x_{2}-x_{1})\\); write \\(x_{2}=x_{1}+kq\\) with \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\).  \nThen \\(y_{2}=y_{1}+kp\\). Thus any two lattice points of the line differ by a non‑zero integer multiple of the primitive vector \\((q,p)\\).\n\nAssume the line contains three distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThen there exist integers \\(k_{1}\\neq k_{2}\\) such that the three points are  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0},y_{0}),\\;(x_{0}+k_{1}q,\\;y_{0}+k_{1}p),\\;(x_{0}+k_{2}q,\\;y_{0}+k_{2}p).\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\); consequently \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \nThe sum of the coordinates of the third point equals  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+y_{0}+k_{2}(p+q).\n\\]\n\nAll three points belong to \\(T_{n}\\), so each sum is at most \\(n+1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+y_{0}+k_{1}|p+q|\\le n+1,\\qquad\nx_{0}+y_{0}+k_{2}|p+q|\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives \\((k_{2}-k_{1})|p+q|\\le0\\), impossible because \\(k_{2}\\neq k_{1}\\) and \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_{n}\\); it contains at most two. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((a,c)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nA vertical line is analogous.  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); intersecting with the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives at most \\(n\\) lattice points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  \nProcess the lines in any order and count, for each line, how many *new* points of \\(T_{n}\\) it contributes (points not already covered by earlier lines).\n\n* a sunny line can contribute at most \\(2\\) new points (Lemma 1);\n* a non‑sunny line can contribute at most \\(n\\) new points (Lemma 2).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}+k(2-n)\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\le k(n-2)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\).\n\n#### 1. Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\nL_{j}:\\;y=j\\;(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nAll are distinct and none is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(L_{0}\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by these \\(n-k\\) lines are\n\n\\[\nS_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid b\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(T_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nS_{k}= \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad |S_{k}|=2k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The equality \\(|S_{k}|=2k\\) follows by a direct count: for each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are exactly two.)\n\n#### 2. Sunny lines covering \\(S_{k}\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\qquad Q_{i}=(i,\\,n-k+i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(S_{k}\\) and are distinct.  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12\\,x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n* The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n* Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n-k+i\\) and \\(y=n-k+i-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\(Q_{i}\\) and \\(P_{i}\\).  \n* Any other lattice point on \\(S_{i}\\) would be of the form \\(P_{i}+m(2,1)\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Adding \\((2,1)\\) increases the sum of coordinates by \\(3\\), which would exceed the bound \\(n+1\\); subtracting \\((2,1)\\) would make a coordinate non‑positive. Therefore \\(P_{i},Q_{i}\\) are the **only** points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThe intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), so the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 3. The whole family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{F}\\) contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies either on one of the horizontal (or diagonal) non‑sunny lines or on one of the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\); thus the covering requirement is satisfied.\n\nSince the construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\), the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n∎"
